/// ML SURFACE GAP (2026-07-14, seed rcdzc reader). A tuple TYPE written with the value/pattern spelling
/// `(A, B)` in a parameter (or any type) annotation is REJECTED — CDZ0203 "a parameter's annotation
/// requires a type, but found a non-type". The ML reader lowers a paren-comma form in TYPE position to
/// the VALUE constructor head `("tuple" A B)` (the same node a tuple LITERAL builds), not the tuple TYPE
/// `(Tuple A B)`, so the annotation resolves to a value where a type is required.
///
///   def f(p: (Int64, Int64)) = p.0    // → CDZ0203 (this file, if uncommented)
///   def g(p: Tuple(Int64, Int64)) = p.0   // OK — `Tuple(...)` is the accepted spelling
///
/// So tuple VALUES and PATTERNS use `(a, b)`, but a tuple TYPE must use `Tuple(A, B)` — an inconsistency
/// (a value literal `(1, 2)` and its type `(Int64, Int64)` do NOT share surface syntax, unlike lists
/// where `[1, 2]` : `List(Int64)` at least keeps the bracket for the literal). A user who writes the
/// natural `(Int64, Int64)` for a pair type gets a confusing "not a type" error pointing at what LOOKS
/// like a type. Ideal: accept `(A, B)` in type position as `Tuple(A, B)` (the reader already knows it is
/// a type annotation — the RHS of a `:` — so a paren-comma there is unambiguously a tuple type).
///
/// This file uses the WORKING `Tuple(...)` spelling so it CHECKS CLEAN (a regression witness for the
/// accepted form); the rejected `(Int64, Int64)` form is shown in the comment above.
def f(p: Tuple(Int64, Int64)) = p.0 + p.1

def pair-sum(a: Int64, b: Int64) = f((a, b))

@test
def tuple-type-param-works() = if pair-sum(3, 4) == 7 then unit else trap("3+4=7 through a Tuple param")

export { f }
